node_modules/
build/
dist/
coverage/
.DS_Store
*.log
.env*
!.env.example

# OS and editor droppings. `.DS_Store` above was the only one covered, which is
# the wrong half for this project: development happens on Windows, where
# Explorer writes `Thumbs.db` into any folder whose images it has previewed and
# `desktop.ini` into any folder given a custom icon. Neither has ever been
# committed here -- these are a backstop, in the same spirit as the credential
# patterns further down, not a response to an incident.
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
*.swp
*.swo
.idea/

# Backup and scratch copies. The failure this prevents is `git add .` after an
# editor or a merge has left `App.tsx.orig` next to `App.tsx` -- a 178 KB near
# duplicate of the largest file in the repo, committed by accident and then
# read by someone as if it were real. Archives are here for the same reason: a
# .zip in a source tree is almost always a download nobody meant to keep.
*.bak
*.old
*.orig
*.rej
*.tmp
*.zip
*.tar
*.tar.gz
*.tgz
*.rar
*.7z

# Editor state, not project data: a single ~22 MB .pbtxt that nothing reads and
# nothing builds from. It matched no pattern here before, so a first `git add .`
# would have committed it and put 22 MB into every clone forever.
.antigravity/

# Credentials pasted into the working tree by mistake. A Gemini key was once
# committed here via envPrefix and revoked by Google's secret scanner; a plain
# key.txt in the repo root is the same accident with fewer steps. These patterns
# are a backstop, not permission — keys belong in the app's Settings dialog,
# which stores them in sessionStorage and never on disk.
key.txt
*.key
*apikey*
*api-key*
secrets.json

# Firebase CLI/emulator working files. `firestore-debug.log` and
# `firebase-debug.log` are already covered by *.log above; `.firebase/` holds
# deploy caches and emulator exports and is not.
#
# `.firebaserc` is deliberately NOT ignored — it names the deploy target
# (forma-201ba) and holds no credentials. Ignoring it is how a fresh clone or a
# CI run ends up deploying to whatever project the CLI last happened to select.
.firebase/

# .NET build output from tools/RepxDesigner. A bare `bin/` would be too broad in
# a JS repo, so both are scoped to tools/. Release is ~137 MB, because the
# DevExpress assemblies are copied local so the tool runs standalone.
tools/**/bin/
tools/**/obj/

# Quarantine. `_not_required/` is where anything unnecessary goes; it is not part
# of the project and nothing may import from it. The contents are ignored so the
# bytes leave the clone, but MANIFEST.md and README.md stay tracked, because the
# *record* of what was parked and why is worth more long-term than the files.
# Git history is the real recovery mechanism; the folder is the convenient one.
#
# The pattern is `_not_required/*`, not `_not_required/`, and that is deliberate:
# git cannot re-include a file whose parent directory is excluded, so ignoring
# the directory itself would make both negations below silently do nothing.
#
# Until 2026-08-28 this folder was deliberately *tracked*, so a grep reached the
# four files parked in it. That is no longer true -- find them through
# MANIFEST.md or `git log`, not through a grep of the working tree.
_not_required/*
!_not_required/MANIFEST.md
!_not_required/README.md
